00:02:57.940 --> 00:02:59.940
He's been thinking about things like

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:02.520
susceptibility to substance
use and aspects of violence.

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:04.340
And for me, I was really just

00:03:05.210 --> 00:03:07.400
to be honest, I was completely,

00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:09.270
I don't want to say
enamored, but surprised

00:03:09.270 --> 00:03:11.100
to see someone of your stature that was so

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:12.870
invested in the community.

00:03:12.870 --> 00:03:14.110
And I know a lot of that was done through

00:03:14.110 --> 00:03:15.490
the Grady project.

00:03:15.490 --> 00:03:18.000
He was at Emory for 18 years
and was leading that project.

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:19.290
I'm sure we'll hear more about that today.

00:03:19.290 --> 00:03:21.670
But just deeply grateful that you've been

00:03:21.670 --> 00:03:22.880
doing this work for so long.

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:24.960
And I'm grateful that
both of you are wanting to

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:27.030
come on here together and
have this conversation

00:03:27.030 --> 00:03:27.863
with one another.

00:03:27.863 --> 00:03:29.770
So, welcome to the program, Dr. Ressler.

00:03:29.770 --> 00:03:30.750
- Thanks so much Dr. Addy.

00:03:30.750 --> 00:03:31.750
It's really a pleasure and really

00:03:31.750 --> 00:03:33.494
looking forward to the conversation.

00:03:33.494 --> 00:03:35.790
- And clearly this is
something that is a topic

00:03:35.790 --> 00:03:38.020
of interest to us as a society.

00:03:38.020 --> 00:03:39.918
I know that even in my own experiences

00:03:39.918 --> 00:03:42.660
as I've been hosting different
events over the years

00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:44.000
it's a phrase that comes up

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:45.790
much more frequently than in the past.

00:03:45.790 --> 00:03:48.510
So people will talk
about, you know, the fact

00:03:48.510 --> 00:03:51.380
that trauma can be passed
on at the molecular level.

00:03:51.380 --> 00:03:53.130
That's something that I
think we can really unpack

00:03:53.130 --> 00:03:54.630
in this conversation.

00:03:54.630 --> 00:03:56.080
But even before we go there

00:03:56.080 --> 00:03:58.280
I do like to often just
check in with guests

00:03:58.280 --> 00:04:00.210
and see how you're both doing.

00:04:00.210 --> 00:04:01.780
Obviously there's a lot that's happening

00:04:01.780 --> 00:04:02.940
in our society right now.

00:04:02.940 --> 00:04:05.360
We're in the middle of the Chauvin trial

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:08.010
and all the emotions that brings up.

00:04:08.010 --> 00:04:10.640
We're continuing to deal with
all the racial injustices.

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:12.767
We're seeing the continuation and

00:04:12.767 --> 00:04:17.180
upsurge of anti-Asian racism.

00:04:17.180 --> 00:04:18.233
And so, I think it's a lot
for us to deal with and

00:04:18.233 --> 00:04:21.040
I would just like to
see how people are doing

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:23.050
in the midst of everything
that's going on.

00:04:23.050 --> 00:04:25.070
So Bianca, if you'd be willing to jump in

00:04:25.070 --> 00:04:27.590
and start at that place.

00:04:27.590 --> 00:04:29.120
- Yes, I am.

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:32.220
I think we're starting off with a very

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:34.660
heavy and loaded question.

00:04:34.660 --> 00:04:35.493
I think we usually pass it.

00:04:35.493 --> 00:04:37.180
We say like, "Oh, we're doing great."

00:04:37.180 --> 00:04:39.660
I can't say I'm doing 100%

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:41.227
As you asked that question,
I pinched my fingers

00:04:41.227 --> 00:04:42.440
so that I don't get too emotional.

00:04:42.440 --> 00:04:44.420
It's a lot, it's very loaded right now.

00:04:44.420 --> 00:04:47.790
I don't think that everyone
is always aware of that.

00:04:47.790 --> 00:04:50.070
And so I think for some
of us who would affect

00:04:50.070 --> 00:04:53.940
or maybe, I would hope that it
would affect everyone equally

00:04:53.940 --> 00:04:58.840
but for those who seem
to feel a stronger pull

00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:00.240
towards social injustice,

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:01.380
it is a little bit hard.

00:05:01.380 --> 00:05:04.300
So it's been a rough
few days, I would say.

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:05.133
- Yeah.

00:05:05.133 --> 00:05:06.790
Well, I definitely appreciate your honesty

00:05:06.790 --> 00:05:09.000
in sharing that as well,
because I think that

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:11.310
that is part of what
we're trying to do here.

00:05:11.310 --> 00:05:13.507
Not to just have that passive,

00:05:13.507 --> 00:05:14.710
"Oh, everything's fine.

00:05:14.710 --> 00:05:15.543
Everything's fine."

00:05:15.543 --> 00:05:16.376
You know.

00:05:16.376 --> 00:05:18.810
And there's a reason
that we go to that place.

00:05:18.810 --> 00:05:20.730
I mean, there is a level
of vulnerability that comes

00:05:20.730 --> 00:05:24.481
and we have to know
that people are actually

00:05:24.481 --> 00:05:26.210
willing and ready to listen.

00:05:26.210 --> 00:05:29.670
But here, I think it's so
important to just be honest.

00:05:29.670 --> 00:05:31.560
And not to detour too
early in the conversation

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:35.460
but I think for many of us
who are in educational spaces

00:05:35.460 --> 00:05:37.408
is that's important for our
students and trainees too

00:05:37.408 --> 00:05:40.120
to not pretend that we're
doing better than we are

00:05:40.120 --> 00:05:41.947
and to create the space
for them to be able to say,

00:05:41.947 --> 00:05:43.253
"Okay, they're also struggling."

00:05:43.253 --> 00:05:46.650
And that actually builds
the community as well.

00:05:46.650 --> 00:05:47.483
So I appreciate you just-

00:05:47.483 --> 00:05:48.380
(voices overlapping)

00:05:48.380 --> 00:05:49.505
- Exactly, not just

00:05:49.505 --> 00:05:51.250
(indistinct)

00:05:51.250 --> 00:05:52.120
- Yeah. Yeah.

00:05:52.120 --> 00:05:53.370
So important.

00:05:53.370 --> 00:05:54.203
What about you, Kerry?

00:05:54.203 --> 00:05:55.036
How are you?

00:05:55.036 --> 00:05:57.050
How are you managing
with everything so far?

00:05:57.050 --> 00:05:58.140
- Yeah, I think, you know,

00:05:58.140 --> 00:06:00.920
it's been quite a year in so many ways

00:06:00.920 --> 00:06:05.010
from all of the, you know I think from the

00:06:05.010 --> 00:06:06.050
racial justice movements

00:06:06.050 --> 00:06:07.970
and Black Lives Matter movement.

00:06:07.970 --> 00:06:09.800
I think, for those of us who,

00:06:09.800 --> 00:06:11.530
in many ways, those of
us in mental health,

00:06:11.530 --> 00:06:13.067
I think are eternally optimistic.

00:06:13.067 --> 00:06:13.913
(Kerry chuckles)

00:06:13.913 --> 00:06:15.900
It feels like maybe this is the time.

00:06:15.900 --> 00:06:17.420
It feels like, you know, since

00:06:17.420 --> 00:06:19.790
at every step along the last 200 years

00:06:19.790 --> 00:06:21.690
and certainly through the 60s
and Civil Rights Movements

00:06:21.690 --> 00:06:23.067
and through the 70s, it can feel like,

00:06:23.067 --> 00:06:25.220
"Okay, we're making progress now."

00:06:25.220 --> 00:06:26.639
And hopefully we are, but
there's certainly many

00:06:26.639 --> 00:06:29.370
many places of systemic racism and

00:06:30.260 --> 00:06:31.502
institutionalized racism that we

00:06:31.502 --> 00:06:32.860
haven't figured it out yet.

00:06:32.860 --> 00:06:34.603
And I grew up in Mississippi.

00:06:36.494 --> 00:06:39.840
And so I think we, as those of us who,

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:41.930
I think all of us have
different levels of privilege

00:06:41.930 --> 00:06:44.520
but certainly, you know, very aware of

00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:48.240
how I, and we as leadership
need to do better.

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:50.580
And so, I think it's such a call to action

00:06:50.580 --> 00:06:52.590
and I just hope that we can really

00:06:52.590 --> 00:06:55.310
make it matter in more, certain more

00:06:55.310 --> 00:06:56.380
important ways this time.

00:06:56.380 --> 00:06:59.080
So, thank you for hosting
this and for this conversation

00:06:59.080 --> 00:07:00.897
'cause this is, I think,
a wonderful way to

00:07:00.897 --> 00:07:03.130
explore these issues in
a much more deep way.

00:07:03.130 --> 00:07:04.030
- Mm.

00:07:04.030 --> 00:07:05.337
Yeah, I definitely appreciate that.

00:07:05.337 --> 00:07:07.980
And I appreciate your acknowledgement

00:07:07.980 --> 00:07:09.830
of the privilege that comes with that too.

00:07:09.830 --> 00:07:12.340
In a way, you're saying both
privilege and responsibility.

00:07:12.340 --> 00:07:13.580
So not just privilege
- Absolutely.

00:07:13.580 --> 00:07:16.000
- But to actually take
steps to move forward.

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:18.170
And at the same time, it
sounds like you're trying to be

00:07:18.170 --> 00:07:20.860
I mean, in a lot of ways,
you're both being realistic.

00:07:20.860 --> 00:07:22.200
There are aspects of pain.

00:07:22.200 --> 00:07:23.033
That's real.

00:07:23.033 --> 00:07:23.866
We can't avoid that.

00:07:23.866 --> 00:07:25.317
There are aspects of optimism but

00:07:25.317 --> 00:07:27.450
there are also aspects of

00:07:27.450 --> 00:07:28.610
we've done this before and

00:07:28.610 --> 00:07:31.290
let's not get stuck in that same cycle

00:07:31.290 --> 00:07:33.950
of just kind of repeating the lip service

00:07:33.950 --> 00:07:36.260
and just going back to
the way things were.

00:07:36.260 --> 00:07:37.867
So I think that's so important.

00:07:37.867 --> 00:07:40.420
And I appreciate your
acknowledgement of what we're

00:07:40.420 --> 00:07:42.450
trying to do with this podcast as well

00:07:42.450 --> 00:07:44.490
to make sure that we can really talk about

00:07:44.490 --> 00:07:46.100
the things that aren't
always talked about.

00:07:46.100 --> 00:07:47.640
Just kind of get,

00:07:47.640 --> 00:07:49.070
I don't want to say
buried under the surface,

00:07:49.070 --> 00:07:50.657
but get left kind of, in that space of,

00:07:50.657 --> 00:07:52.457
'well, we don't really
want to touch that.'

00:07:52.457 --> 00:07:54.980
And Bianca, I know
you've done a lot of that

00:07:54.980 --> 00:07:56.920
over the last year as well.

00:07:56.920 --> 00:07:58.080
Kerry, I know through our interactions

00:07:58.080 --> 00:07:59.100
that's been the focus for you.

00:07:59.100 --> 00:08:00.510
So again, I'm just grateful,

00:08:00.510 --> 00:08:02.070
grateful to both of you

00:08:02.070 --> 00:08:03.970
for the way that you're pulling
all those things together.

00:08:03.970 --> 00:08:06.803
It's not easy, not easy
in any sense of the word.

00:08:07.840 --> 00:08:10.180
So Kerry, you hinted a little
bit about your background.

00:08:10.180 --> 00:08:12.150
I did wanna dig in a
little bit deeper there

00:08:12.150 --> 00:08:13.190
and actually ask both of you

00:08:13.190 --> 00:08:17.260
how you got to this place in your lives of

00:08:17.260 --> 00:08:19.920
being deeply invested
in neuroscience research

00:08:19.920 --> 00:08:23.260
especially for those in our
audience who aren't scientists.

00:08:23.260 --> 00:08:24.380
I'm sure they'd be curious to hear

00:08:24.380 --> 00:08:27.690
why are the two of you so
deeply invested in this area?

00:08:27.690 --> 00:08:29.290
So Kerry, if you could
talk a little bit about

00:08:29.290 --> 00:08:31.730
your journey, I guess both to medicine

00:08:31.730 --> 00:08:32.880
and to neuroscience research.

00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:34.930
How did you come to that place?

00:08:34.930 --> 00:08:35.763
- Sure.

00:08:36.982 --> 00:08:38.690
For a brief background.

00:08:38.690 --> 00:08:40.040
So I grew up in Mississippi

00:08:40.920 --> 00:08:42.820
for multiple generations in the South

00:08:42.820 --> 00:08:44.470
and first generation college

00:08:44.470 --> 00:08:46.750
from a single parent household.

00:08:46.750 --> 00:08:49.680
So although I was white, my mom grew up in

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:52.240
segregationist Mississippi
and her best friends

00:08:52.240 --> 00:08:54.270
were black, where she
lived in the country.

00:08:54.270 --> 00:08:58.772
And so I really heard the
stories of what that was like.

00:08:58.772 --> 00:09:01.070
So I was always very aware, you know

00:09:01.070 --> 00:09:05.910
had a very progressive mother
in early 70s Mississippi.

00:09:05.910 --> 00:09:10.090
And so was very aware of
racial injustice from there.

00:09:10.090 --> 00:09:12.480
So that side, and then I was also

00:09:12.480 --> 00:09:14.440
working in a grocery store and

00:09:14.440 --> 00:09:16.000
everything else to just get by.

00:09:16.000 --> 00:09:17.820
And fortunately, had great math teachers

00:09:17.820 --> 00:09:20.530
and great teachers and had
the opportunity to do well.

00:09:20.530 --> 00:09:21.650
And I was sort of a child of

00:09:21.650 --> 00:09:23.350
the first generation of computers.

00:09:25.253 --> 00:09:29.047
So I was applying for college
and my math teacher said,

00:09:29.047 --> 00:09:30.160
"You should look at MIT"

00:09:30.160 --> 00:09:31.995
and I'm like, "What is MIT?"

00:09:31.995 --> 00:09:33.291
(laughs)

00:09:33.291 --> 00:09:35.017
And so I was fortunate
enough to get into MIT

00:09:35.017 --> 00:09:36.810
and I went there to be
a computer scientist

00:09:36.810 --> 00:09:38.360
as I thought.

00:09:38.360 --> 00:09:41.250
I'm still convinced that I got into MIT

00:09:41.250 --> 00:09:42.690
because I was the token Mississippi person

00:09:42.690 --> 00:09:44.009
but I'll take it.

00:09:44.009 --> 00:09:46.090
(laughs)

00:09:46.090 --> 00:09:47.627
But along the way,

00:09:47.627 --> 00:09:49.640
I was already exposed for the first time

00:09:49.640 --> 00:09:51.120
to molecular biology neuroscience

00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:53.800
and just absolutely fell in love with it.

00:09:53.800 --> 00:09:55.190
In the 80s, seeing this sort of

00:09:55.190 --> 00:09:57.350
molecular generate revolution

00:09:57.350 --> 00:09:59.820
that you can really engineer biology

00:09:59.820 --> 00:10:00.810
seemed much more exciting to me

00:10:00.810 --> 00:10:03.670
than engineering program computers.

00:10:03.670 --> 00:10:05.580
So, long story short.

00:10:05.580 --> 00:10:06.413
Did that.

00:10:06.413 --> 00:10:09.470
Was in on the fence between
neuroscience and medicine.

00:10:09.470 --> 00:10:11.823
I worked in emergency departments
and worked in medicine.

00:10:11.823 --> 00:10:14.040
Just really wanted to
give back to the community

00:10:14.040 --> 00:10:15.360
but loved research.

00:10:15.360 --> 00:10:16.550
So I was fortunate enough to get into

00:10:16.550 --> 00:10:19.160
an MD-PhD program and
stay in Boston at Harvard.

00:10:19.160 --> 00:10:20.950
And that's where I met Linda Buck.

00:10:20.950 --> 00:10:23.360
I was doing research.

00:10:23.360 --> 00:10:24.900
I was doing my rotations

00:10:24.900 --> 00:10:27.220
for what I was going to do my PhD in

00:10:27.220 --> 00:10:29.010
and couldn't quite find the right one.

00:10:29.010 --> 00:10:31.520
I wanted to do plasticity
and learning and memory.

00:10:31.520 --> 00:10:33.330
And there weren't many people
at Harvard at the time.

00:10:33.330 --> 00:10:34.680
And then somebody said,
"You should come hear this

00:10:34.680 --> 00:10:37.010
job talk by this Buck person."

00:10:37.010 --> 00:10:40.280
And so I went, not knowing
anything about smell in my life

00:10:40.280 --> 00:10:42.100
and Linda Buck and Richard Axel

00:10:42.100 --> 00:10:45.120
who you'll hear from
Bianca, had just discovered

00:10:45.120 --> 00:10:46.390
the odor receptor gene family.

00:10:46.390 --> 00:10:48.510
And that just opened my
eyes to a whole way that one

00:10:48.510 --> 00:10:51.970
could use molecular tools to
understand a sensory system.

00:10:51.970 --> 00:10:53.980
And it really, for the
last 30 years since then

00:10:53.980 --> 00:10:56.150
been using molecular tools to understand

00:10:56.150 --> 00:10:58.160
systems' neuroscience questions.

00:10:58.160 --> 00:11:00.060
And as we get into the
later discussion about

00:11:00.060 --> 00:11:01.340
some of the intergenerational stuff,

00:11:01.340 --> 00:11:03.723
it was that background in olfaction

00:11:03.723 --> 00:11:04.690
that was really exciting.

00:11:04.690 --> 00:11:07.650
So, from there did a
post-doc at Emory in Atlanta

00:11:07.650 --> 00:11:09.850
and moved to Atlanta
for a number of reasons.

00:11:09.850 --> 00:11:10.683
It's a great program.

00:11:10.683 --> 00:11:13.340
My wife was from there and we had a baby.

00:11:13.340 --> 00:11:14.530
All those things.

00:11:14.530 --> 00:11:16.590
And Grady Hospital, one of the main

00:11:16.590 --> 00:11:17.710
teaching hospitals in a way, and

00:11:17.710 --> 00:11:20.330
I really wanted to give
back and train at Grady.

00:11:20.330 --> 00:11:23.530
And so, while both working
with Michael Davis there

00:11:23.530 --> 00:11:25.950
on fear, and he was one of the founders

00:11:25.950 --> 00:11:27.030
of the amygdala systems,

00:11:27.030 --> 00:11:29.090
I started using molecular
biology approaches

00:11:29.090 --> 00:11:30.950
to understanding the amygdala and fear.

00:11:30.950 --> 00:11:32.880
Also was approached

00:11:32.880 --> 00:11:34.570
began to understand what PTSD was

00:11:34.570 --> 00:11:36.410
in the veterans' populations.

00:11:36.410 --> 00:11:38.910
And in my work as a psychiatrist at Grady

00:11:38.910 --> 00:11:40.520
really saw that trauma was everywhere

00:11:40.520 --> 00:11:41.867
and yet we weren't paying attention to it.

00:11:41.867 --> 00:11:44.450
And so that really began
our 20-year journey

00:11:44.450 --> 00:11:46.680
into understanding
trauma in the inner city.

00:11:46.680 --> 00:11:49.840
And since then, we've
interviewed almost 13,000 people

00:11:49.840 --> 00:11:52.310
coming to Grady hospital
just for general medical care

00:11:52.310 --> 00:11:53.990
in that process and learned a lot about

00:11:53.990 --> 00:11:56.400
trauma exposure, just the enormity of it.

00:11:56.400 --> 00:11:59.370
The enormity of PTSD and
depression and co-morbidity.

00:11:59.370 --> 00:12:01.210
So really, I've spent a lot of my years

00:12:01.210 --> 00:12:02.070
in trying to understand

00:12:02.070 --> 00:12:03.830
by understanding the medical model

00:12:03.830 --> 00:12:05.290
and neuroscience approach to trauma

00:12:05.290 --> 00:12:06.640
and that really helps us have

00:12:06.640 --> 00:12:08.600
better interventions and improvements.

00:12:08.600 --> 00:12:09.433
So that's me.

00:12:09.433 --> 00:12:10.863
Otherwise, I've been married for 30 years.

00:12:10.863 --> 00:12:12.952
I have a couple dogs and three boys.

00:12:12.952 --> 00:12:13.785
(Addy chuckles)

00:12:13.785 --> 00:12:16.420
and I'm very grateful
for the life I've had.

00:12:16.420 --> 00:12:17.529
Thank you.

00:12:17.529 --> 00:12:18.370
- That's great.

00:12:18.370 --> 00:12:19.730
Well, thanks for sharing the journey.

00:12:19.730 --> 00:12:21.460
I mean, even going all the
way back to the beginning.

00:12:21.460 --> 00:12:22.670
That was very impressive by the way,

00:12:22.670 --> 00:12:25.240
the way you succinctly encapsulate

00:12:25.240 --> 00:12:28.240
so many aspects of your life story.

00:12:28.240 --> 00:12:29.073
But I appreciate that.

00:12:29.073 --> 00:12:30.330
I think it's helpful for people to be able

00:12:30.330 --> 00:12:32.560
to just get the the entire context too.

00:12:32.560 --> 00:12:34.400
I mean, even going back
to your family history

00:12:34.400 --> 00:12:36.820
and your first-generation experiences

00:12:36.820 --> 00:12:40.100
and the opportunities that
you're able to take advantage of.

00:12:40.100 --> 00:12:41.540
Definitely appreciate that.

00:12:41.540 --> 00:12:43.630
I know we'll tie back and
think even more deeply

00:12:43.630 --> 00:12:44.769
about some of the work
that you're currently

00:12:44.769 --> 00:12:46.910
doing now as well.

00:12:46.910 --> 00:12:49.100
But just grateful for the
investment that you've made

00:12:49.100 --> 00:12:50.498
in a lot of ways.

00:12:50.498 --> 00:12:51.337
- Thank you.

00:12:51.337 --> 00:12:52.480
- And Bianca, what about you?

00:12:52.480 --> 00:12:54.060
I know you shared some of your story

00:12:54.060 --> 00:12:55.760
in other settings before, both in terms of

00:12:55.760 --> 00:12:58.940
your scientific journey
and your family background

00:12:58.940 --> 00:13:01.410
growing up, and your
thoughts about teaching

00:13:01.410 --> 00:13:03.560
how all that led you to this place.

00:13:03.560 --> 00:13:04.710
So curious to have you expand

00:13:04.710 --> 00:13:06.110
on that a little bit here as well.

00:13:06.110 --> 00:13:07.830
- Yes, I'm so happy to expand.

00:13:07.830 --> 00:13:11.240
I'm a little bit overwhelmed at the

00:13:11.240 --> 00:13:15.512
amazing parallel between Kerry and myself.

00:13:15.512 --> 00:13:18.630
I don't think, just
taking a brief look at us.

00:13:18.630 --> 00:13:20.470
I mean, if there's anything
that's similar outside of

00:13:20.470 --> 00:13:23.110
we're both gray hair color-wise, but

00:13:24.060 --> 00:13:25.483
I'm so taken aback.

00:13:26.478 --> 00:13:27.530
And so thank you so much for sharing,

00:13:27.530 --> 00:13:30.060
especially like some
aspects that I have found

00:13:30.060 --> 00:13:31.980
as I continue on in science that

00:13:31.980 --> 00:13:35.505
are sometimes hard to share openly

00:13:35.505 --> 00:13:37.640
like struggles coming
from a single family home

00:13:37.640 --> 00:13:39.900
coming from like, maybe a low SES.

00:13:39.900 --> 00:13:41.413
I've been very hesitant
to share these things.

00:13:41.413 --> 00:13:43.420
Just only as of recently
that I realized that

00:13:43.420 --> 00:13:46.350
in me sharing this, I'm
inspiring people who

00:13:46.350 --> 00:13:48.660
don't know that they
assume differently about me

00:13:48.660 --> 00:13:51.620
that like, "Oh, they
can actually do this."

00:13:51.620 --> 00:13:52.453
It's possible.

00:13:52.453 --> 00:13:53.286
Not hard, but possible.

00:13:53.286 --> 00:13:56.570
So I am a New Yorker, through and through.

00:13:56.570 --> 00:13:58.350
Born and raised.

00:13:58.350 --> 00:13:59.183
Very proud of that.

00:13:59.183 --> 00:14:00.660
So I can't pronounce words like drawer.

00:14:00.660 --> 00:14:01.600
It's totally fine.

00:14:01.600 --> 00:14:03.010
We say it too often in science

00:14:03.010 --> 00:14:03.910
and everyone's always confused.

00:14:03.910 --> 00:14:05.330
It's fine.

00:14:05.330 --> 00:14:07.950
So, I'm from New York, born in Queens

00:14:07.950 --> 00:14:11.050
and I'm first generation
American on one side.

00:14:11.050 --> 00:14:13.130
My mother was born in
Guyana, South America.

00:14:13.130 --> 00:14:14.850
My dad is a New Yorker,
through and through

00:14:14.850 --> 00:14:16.180
for a few generations.

00:14:16.180 --> 00:14:17.740
Although I do have family from my

00:14:17.740 --> 00:14:20.060
outside of Birmingham,
my grandmother was born

00:14:20.060 --> 00:14:21.240
right outside of Birmingham.

00:14:21.240 --> 00:14:22.817
So, one side is black American,

00:14:22.817 --> 00:14:25.015
and the other side is Guyanese.

00:14:25.015 --> 00:14:26.030
And,

00:14:26.030 --> 00:14:28.200
I, from my perspective

00:14:28.200 --> 00:14:31.030
I had an extremely happy
and amazing childhood.

00:14:31.030 --> 00:14:32.810
And I do think that
it's only in retrospect

00:14:32.810 --> 00:14:33.760
that I realized how

00:14:34.620 --> 00:14:39.620
uniquely set up my childhood
was in inspiring what I do now.

00:14:40.160 --> 00:14:41.640
So I grew up in a,

00:14:41.640 --> 00:14:43.618
my parents, my biological parents

00:14:43.618 --> 00:14:45.660
but they were foster parents as well.

00:14:45.660 --> 00:14:48.020
So I have foster siblings
and adopted siblings

00:14:48.860 --> 00:14:51.780
which allowed me insight
into other people's

00:14:51.780 --> 00:14:55.710
world and life, without
ever, like I'm so thankful

00:14:55.710 --> 00:14:57.340
without ever having to experience it

00:14:57.340 --> 00:14:58.950
because a lot of these things
are associated with trauma.

00:14:58.950 --> 00:15:00.940
If you are in the foster care system,

00:15:00.940 --> 00:15:05.240
it's because you have a
caregiver who wasn't able to

00:15:05.240 --> 00:15:07.370
live up to that definition of caregiver

00:15:07.370 --> 00:15:09.510
and in no shame associated
with the caregiver.

00:15:09.510 --> 00:15:11.020
And I feel that very strongly.

00:15:11.020 --> 00:15:12.830
That's also why I study parental behavior

00:15:12.830 --> 00:15:14.110
because we can't always say it's like

00:15:14.110 --> 00:15:16.300
the parent's issue or the child's issue.

00:15:16.300 --> 00:15:18.014
There's a lot of things
involved with that.

00:15:18.014 --> 00:15:19.790
But I was able to get
a little bit of insight

00:15:19.790 --> 00:15:23.040
into how it is to live in a
home that wasn't like mine.

00:15:23.040 --> 00:15:25.770
And I think that really motivated
me to go into education.

00:15:25.770 --> 00:15:27.980
So I first started off as a teacher.

00:15:27.980 --> 00:15:29.940
It's amazing, like Kerry said.

00:15:29.940 --> 00:15:31.810
Someone said, "Go check out MIT."

00:15:31.810 --> 00:15:33.750
And you're like, "What is MIT?"

00:15:33.750 --> 00:15:36.450
I started going into
education and I saw a sign

00:15:36.450 --> 00:15:38.480
that said like, do one, in college.

00:15:38.480 --> 00:15:39.590
I went to St. John's in Queens.

00:15:39.590 --> 00:15:40.450
I literally have never

00:15:40.450 --> 00:15:41.409
I haven't left New York

00:15:41.409 --> 00:15:42.242
(Bianca chuckles)

00:15:42.242 --> 00:15:44.900
My undergrad, grad school,
postdoc is all here.

00:15:44.900 --> 00:15:49.900
And I saw a flyer that said
you can go to get free tuition.

00:15:50.060 --> 00:15:50.957
You work in a lab.

00:15:50.957 --> 00:15:51.890
I was like, "That sounds great."

00:15:51.890 --> 00:15:53.370
I was an education major.

00:15:53.370 --> 00:15:55.030
And I started doing fungal genetics

00:15:55.030 --> 00:15:58.470
and it entered me into
the world of science.

00:15:58.470 --> 00:16:00.644
They ask what does a PhD stand for?

00:16:00.644 --> 00:16:01.640
And I was like,
(mumbles)

00:16:01.640 --> 00:16:02.473
I didn't know what to say before,

00:16:02.473 --> 00:16:04.213
and I learned more about PhD.

00:16:06.160 --> 00:16:08.943
Presenting that data at a
conference called ABRCMS

00:16:08.943 --> 00:16:10.940
It's the annual biomedical
research conference

00:16:10.940 --> 00:16:12.320
for minority students.

00:16:12.320 --> 00:16:14.020
I had someone come up to me, a Dean

00:16:14.020 --> 00:16:15.987
and he's like, "Oh, you
should check out my school,

00:16:15.987 --> 00:16:18.330
MIT, for our summer program.

00:16:18.330 --> 00:16:20.170
Being from New York, I know MIT.

00:16:20.170 --> 00:16:22.320
I know NYIT, New York
Institute of Technology

00:16:22.320 --> 00:16:23.540
which like a little school in my town.

00:16:23.540 --> 00:16:25.940
And I was like, "I guess if
I wanna go to Massachusetts.

00:16:25.940 --> 00:16:27.409
Sure, I'll check it out."

00:16:27.409 --> 00:16:28.420
(Bianca chuckles)

00:16:28.420 --> 00:16:30.500
It was that same year
that a movie came out

00:16:30.500 --> 00:16:32.900
called '21', which is about some brilliant

00:16:32.900 --> 00:16:35.440
MIT's mathematicians,

00:16:35.440 --> 00:16:37.810
who go to Vegas and they game the system

00:16:37.810 --> 00:16:38.872
and win a whole lot of money.

00:16:38.872 --> 00:16:39.705
And I was like-

00:16:39.705 --> 00:16:41.251
- I knew some of the people in that group.

00:16:41.251 --> 00:16:42.084
- Did you?

00:16:42.084 --> 00:16:42.917
(laughs)

00:16:42.917 --> 00:16:44.830
- Wow.
- Sorry, go on.

00:16:44.830 --> 00:16:46.290
- MIT's a big deal!

00:16:46.290 --> 00:16:48.530
Oh! I should fill up an application out.

00:16:48.530 --> 00:16:50.600
I did not know at all.
- Oh my goodness.

00:16:50.600 --> 00:16:53.090
- So that was one of my
ways into neuroscience.

00:16:53.090 --> 00:16:55.490
I worked in a lab there for the summer.

00:16:55.490 --> 00:16:56.323
I learned more about neuroscience.

00:16:56.323 --> 00:16:58.150
And I think during that, the path of

00:16:58.150 --> 00:17:00.380
being part of this program which is for

00:17:00.380 --> 00:17:02.880
first generation student, college students

00:17:02.880 --> 00:17:04.430
which I was, the first of my family to go

00:17:04.430 --> 00:17:05.640
to college and graduate.

00:17:05.640 --> 00:17:08.540
First generation Americans, low SES

00:17:08.540 --> 00:17:10.243
all the things I checked off.

00:17:11.400 --> 00:17:13.820
The program that got
me, that introduced me

00:17:13.820 --> 00:17:17.770
to this research, really
allowed me to align

00:17:17.770 --> 00:17:19.610
the fact that I loved teaching.

00:17:19.610 --> 00:17:21.010
I loved the concept of learning.

00:17:21.010 --> 00:17:23.130
I wanted to understand
how environmental factors

00:17:23.130 --> 00:17:25.400
like stress, like
trauma, made it different

00:17:25.400 --> 00:17:27.060
for my siblings to navigate school.

00:17:27.060 --> 00:17:30.000
And for me, I was a pretty
solid, straight A student.

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:32.540
I knew my siblings had the
capabilities to do that

00:17:32.540 --> 00:17:34.420
but I just think
environmental factors did not

00:17:34.420 --> 00:17:36.060
allow them to focus on that

00:17:36.060 --> 00:17:38.210
'cause there was so much more going on.

00:17:38.210 --> 00:17:41.180
And so, within getting
the science component,

00:17:41.180 --> 00:17:43.510
the learning and then being
introduced to neuroscience,

00:17:43.510 --> 00:17:45.200
the summer programs that I went to,

00:17:45.200 --> 00:17:47.650
I decided I wanted to
study learning and memory

00:17:47.650 --> 00:17:49.200
as opposed to being a teacher.

00:17:49.200 --> 00:17:51.270
I did teach 7th grade,
10th grade and AP biology

00:17:51.270 --> 00:17:53.993
for a year before I
entered into grad school.

00:17:56.381 --> 00:17:57.550
For any graduate students listening,

00:17:57.550 --> 00:18:00.650
I had a very interesting
graduate school career.

00:18:00.650 --> 00:18:01.990
I did five rotations.

00:18:01.990 --> 00:18:05.270
Most people do three, in a lab,
to find where you wanna go.

00:18:05.270 --> 00:18:08.330
And the fifth rotation
was with Robert Froemke.

00:18:08.330 --> 00:18:09.600
Dr. Robert Froemke.

00:18:09.600 --> 00:18:11.070
And he was like, "You can
do some of this stuff.

00:18:11.070 --> 00:18:11.903
Some of this stuff.

00:18:11.903 --> 00:18:13.500
I have a slight interest
in maternal behavior."

00:18:13.500 --> 00:18:14.498
And I was like, "Oh, that's me.

00:18:14.498 --> 00:18:15.331
That's what I wanna do.

00:18:15.331 --> 00:18:16.610
I wanna study maternal behavior."

00:18:16.610 --> 00:18:18.630
And it led to the Oxytocin Project that

00:18:18.630 --> 00:18:21.330
a large percentage of his
lab is now looking at.

00:18:21.330 --> 00:18:24.960
And similarly, when it came
to looking into my postdoc

00:18:24.960 --> 00:18:26.690
I had a few offers.

00:18:26.690 --> 00:18:29.230
My PhD work was very well received

00:18:29.230 --> 00:18:33.200
and we found some pretty
amazing groundbreaking results

00:18:33.200 --> 00:18:35.070
that led me to offers to start my own lab

00:18:35.070 --> 00:18:36.420
prior to even doing a postdoc.

00:18:36.420 --> 00:18:39.430
And so I looked into those.

00:18:39.430 --> 00:18:40.950
But on meeting Richard Axel

00:18:40.950 --> 00:18:42.630
who I'm honored doing my post-doc with,

00:18:42.630 --> 00:18:44.350
who is also here at Columbia,

00:18:44.350 --> 00:18:46.673
who won the Nobel prize with Linda Buck,

00:18:47.509 --> 00:18:49.193
who Kerry worked with.

00:18:50.100 --> 00:18:53.050
He, A, convinced me that New
York is where I wanted to stay.

00:18:53.050 --> 00:18:53.963
He's really good at that.

00:18:53.963 --> 00:18:55.500
He's also a New Yorker.

00:18:55.500 --> 00:18:58.130
And B, that I can learn a new facet.

00:18:58.130 --> 00:19:00.640
I also didn't think I would
be interested in odor.

00:19:00.640 --> 00:19:04.290
My former work was auditory.

00:19:04.290 --> 00:19:05.123
So listening.

00:19:05.123 --> 00:19:05.956
It's a beautiful stimulus.

00:19:05.956 --> 00:19:07.960
You play a tone, you see
a response in the brain,

00:19:07.960 --> 00:19:09.940
like humidity doesn't change it.

00:19:09.940 --> 00:19:11.120
You can wear perfume.

00:19:11.120 --> 00:19:12.363
Odor is very different.

00:19:13.440 --> 00:19:15.535
But the questions you can
ask with a beautiful system

00:19:15.535 --> 00:19:17.060
like the olfactory system,

00:19:17.060 --> 00:19:18.237
just opened my mind to

00:19:18.237 --> 00:19:21.960
and my eyes to so many aspects.

00:19:21.960 --> 00:19:25.290
And the fact that we can associate trauma

00:19:25.290 --> 00:19:27.460
and memory with things like

00:19:27.460 --> 00:19:29.040
what grandma was cooking in the kitchen

00:19:29.040 --> 00:19:31.010
and the smell of the apple pie.

00:19:31.010 --> 00:19:33.630
It excites me as to where
we can take this field.

00:19:33.630 --> 00:19:36.300
And so now, I'm an assistant
professor at Columbia

00:19:36.300 --> 00:19:37.650
studying how trauma is passed down

00:19:37.650 --> 00:19:39.790
through generations and
how parents play a role

00:19:39.790 --> 00:19:42.430
in this and how environmental
factors like smell

00:19:42.430 --> 00:19:44.580
and sound and taste and the senses

00:19:44.580 --> 00:19:46.840
take in the world and
keep those memories in

00:19:46.840 --> 00:19:48.190
and pass those memories on.

00:19:49.870 --> 00:19:50.910
- Wow.

00:19:50.910 --> 00:19:51.743
That's so

00:19:52.780 --> 00:19:54.800
inspiring and humbling, at the same time,

00:19:54.800 --> 00:19:55.850
to hear you share your story.

00:19:55.850 --> 00:19:56.683
And I'm struck.

00:19:56.683 --> 00:19:58.050
I mean, you said this yourself, right?

00:19:58.050 --> 00:20:02.480
How many intersections and
overlaps you and Kerry have.

00:20:02.480 --> 00:20:04.520
Even the interjection about the MIT folks.

00:20:04.520 --> 00:20:05.520
(Bianca laughs)

00:20:05.520 --> 00:20:07.870
I mean, there's just, wow.

00:20:07.870 --> 00:20:09.230
So scientifically and otherwise.

00:20:09.230 --> 00:20:11.800
And I think, I mean, that's the beauty of

00:20:11.800 --> 00:20:13.750
the work we do sometimes, I think too.

00:20:13.750 --> 00:20:15.930
And to be honest, I think it also speaks

00:20:15.930 --> 00:20:19.070
to the importance of sometimes
us having conversations

00:20:19.070 --> 00:20:22.200
outside of our specific scientific

00:20:22.200 --> 00:20:23.260
question and discipline,

00:20:23.260 --> 00:20:24.340
not that there's anything wrong with that.

00:20:24.340 --> 00:20:25.870
I mean, we all get
really excited about that

00:20:25.870 --> 00:20:27.320
but there's so much more.

00:20:27.320 --> 00:20:28.530
Sometimes, I think that we don't have

00:20:28.530 --> 00:20:32.080
the chance to appreciate
because we are stuck

00:20:32.080 --> 00:20:33.210
in that kind of framework about

00:20:33.210 --> 00:20:34.660
only speaking about things in one way.

00:20:34.660 --> 00:20:36.470
So, obviously that's a

00:20:36.470 --> 00:20:37.830
maybe that's a self-serving comment

00:20:37.830 --> 00:20:39.760
'cause that's one of the things
that I'm really trying to do

00:20:39.760 --> 00:20:41.530
on the Addy Hour too,
just to be able to pull in

00:20:41.530 --> 00:20:43.100
so many different topics and make sure

00:20:43.100 --> 00:20:45.690
that we can unearth all
those pieces as well.

00:20:45.690 --> 00:20:47.380
So, definitely appreciate you Bianca,

00:20:47.380 --> 00:20:50.660
sharing those components,
and Kerry as well.

00:20:50.660 --> 00:20:52.520
There's so many different
directions that we could go.

00:20:52.520 --> 00:20:53.830
I mean, to be honest, it's almost feels

00:20:53.830 --> 00:20:55.270
like it could be a
three hour conversation.

00:20:55.270 --> 00:20:56.495
- Yeah, it does.

00:20:56.495 --> 00:20:57.494
- We're not gonna do that, but

00:20:57.494 --> 00:20:59.120
(Addy chuckles)

00:20:59.120 --> 00:21:01.110
But one thing that I did want to pull out

00:21:01.110 --> 00:21:03.640
from something you both
mentioned too, is just

00:21:03.640 --> 00:21:05.020
how you got interested,

00:21:05.020 --> 00:21:06.760
and Bianca, you touched
on this already too.

00:21:06.760 --> 00:21:09.930
But about trauma specifically,

00:21:09.930 --> 00:21:12.270
just because clearly
that's something that has

00:21:12.270 --> 00:21:14.130
a lot of societal implications.

00:21:14.130 --> 00:21:16.340
I think in a lot of ways in the last year,

00:21:16.340 --> 00:21:17.290
as a broader society

00:21:17.290 --> 00:21:18.660
both of you have been
thinking about this already

00:21:18.660 --> 00:21:21.170
but we're starting to
pay much more attention

00:21:21.170 --> 00:21:23.680
to that than we have in the past.

00:21:23.680 --> 00:21:25.510
So, I'm curious to hear
even a little bit more

00:21:25.510 --> 00:21:27.410
about how you got invested in that.

00:21:27.410 --> 00:21:30.780
And then if you could
also just unpack this idea

00:21:30.780 --> 00:21:35.100
of the cellular molecular ways
that trauma gets passed on.

00:21:35.100 --> 00:21:36.840
What does that actually mean?

00:21:36.840 --> 00:21:38.480
Because of that phrase, as I mentioned

00:21:38.480 --> 00:21:40.540
gets thrown around a lot.

00:21:40.540 --> 00:21:42.070
And I think it's good that people

00:21:42.070 --> 00:21:42.903
are paying attention to it.

00:21:42.903 --> 00:21:43.930
But I think it's also important

00:21:43.930 --> 00:21:45.667
that we really have a good understanding.

00:21:45.667 --> 00:21:47.530
And to be honest, that's an illness

00:21:47.530 --> 00:21:50.610
that I think we as scientists
have to communicate that.

00:21:50.610 --> 00:21:53.121
So Kerry, if we can go
ahead and start with you.

00:21:53.121 --> 00:21:54.400
- So as you say, we could probably talk

00:21:54.400 --> 00:21:56.160
for 10 hours on those two topics.

00:21:56.160 --> 00:21:57.590
I'll give the very short version

00:21:57.590 --> 00:21:58.735
and we can dive deeper.

00:21:58.735 --> 00:21:59.751
(Kerry chuckles)

00:21:59.751 --> 00:22:00.623
So for me,

00:22:02.030 --> 00:22:05.830
I was interested in
trauma as a psychiatrist

00:22:06.870 --> 00:22:09.380
who wanted to combine

00:22:09.380 --> 00:22:10.280
so it really, two sides.

00:22:10.280 --> 00:22:11.810
One, the intellectual side.

00:22:11.810 --> 00:22:15.670
I found that our
understanding in the late 90s

00:22:15.670 --> 00:22:18.420
of the amygdala and the
brain circuit regions

00:22:18.420 --> 00:22:21.490
that encode the fear and threat responses

00:22:21.490 --> 00:22:23.790
were some of the best
understood brain circuits

00:22:23.790 --> 00:22:25.400
that we had in mammals.

00:22:25.400 --> 00:22:27.990
And so as a physician scientist,

00:22:27.990 --> 00:22:29.430
I really wanted to study something

00:22:29.430 --> 00:22:31.270
that I could both understand
more mechanistically

00:22:31.270 --> 00:22:34.150
in an animal system and understand

00:22:34.150 --> 00:22:36.080
in something relevant to humans.

00:22:36.080 --> 00:22:38.040
So I was very attracted to PTSD

00:22:38.040 --> 00:22:40.000
as a purely intellectual question.

00:22:40.000 --> 00:22:41.840
But I also really, you
know, I went into medicine

00:22:41.840 --> 00:22:45.120
and wanted to do something
related to helping people

00:22:45.120 --> 00:22:48.770
but from both my Mississippi
and earlier backgrounds

00:22:48.770 --> 00:22:50.320
and seeing racial injustice,
I really wanted to

00:22:50.320 --> 00:22:53.370
do something that could
eventually help the community.

00:22:53.370 --> 00:22:55.220
I knew I wasn't gonna be a policy wonk.

00:22:55.220 --> 00:22:56.190
It wasn't really who I was,

00:22:56.190 --> 00:22:58.290
but if I could do something scientifically

00:22:58.290 --> 00:23:00.250
that would speak to community issues

00:23:00.250 --> 00:23:01.640
that would be a lovely
way to combine them.

00:23:01.640 --> 00:23:04.470
And for me, it started
when my eyes were opened up

00:23:04.470 --> 00:23:06.311
working at Grady as an intern

00:23:06.311 --> 00:23:07.870
in the first year of residency

00:23:07.870 --> 00:23:09.894
and was interviewing people in our general

00:23:09.894 --> 00:23:12.650
So Grady hospitals,
the main receiving area

00:23:12.650 --> 00:23:14.450
the main trauma hospital for all of

00:23:14.450 --> 00:23:16.440
Atlanta's five plus million people.

00:23:16.440 --> 00:23:19.600
And they're also the main
psychiatric receiving facility.

00:23:19.600 --> 00:23:22.280
And, you'd be thrown in
there as an early resident

00:23:22.280 --> 00:23:23.770
not really knowing much of anything

00:23:23.770 --> 00:23:25.730
'cause you interview
people and make decisions.

00:23:25.730 --> 00:23:27.450
And what I found first is that nobody

00:23:27.450 --> 00:23:29.730
quite fit our different
diagnostic criteria.

00:23:29.730 --> 00:23:31.800
Everybody, you know,
there was substance abuse

00:23:31.800 --> 00:23:33.940
there was depression,
there was suicidality

00:23:33.940 --> 00:23:36.390
there was psychosis and all
of it was kinda mixed up.

00:23:36.390 --> 00:23:38.107
And certainly substance
was underlying a lot of it.

00:23:38.107 --> 00:23:39.630
But having come from the VA and

00:23:39.630 --> 00:23:42.070
seeing the PTSD in the veterans,

00:23:42.070 --> 00:23:43.140
to me, it was very obvious.

00:23:43.140 --> 00:23:44.640
As soon as we started
asking people about trauma,

00:23:44.640 --> 00:23:48.230
it was everywhere, and it
was almost like in the 1800s

00:23:48.230 --> 00:23:49.597
we called syphilis kind
of the great mimicker.

00:23:49.597 --> 00:23:51.891
And I think trauma's kind
of a great mimicker for it

00:23:51.891 --> 00:23:53.880
whether they're medical comorbidities

00:23:53.880 --> 00:23:56.850
or psychiatric comorbidities
or other components.

00:23:56.850 --> 00:23:58.157
So I just, I was fascinated with it.

00:23:58.157 --> 00:24:01.110
And so for me, being
able to look at trauma

00:24:01.110 --> 00:24:04.190
from both the basic side, understand PTSD

00:24:04.190 --> 00:24:07.290
and start to really help
understand and give voice to

00:24:07.290 --> 00:24:10.440
civilian trauma and in at-risk communities

00:24:10.440 --> 00:24:12.540
and how that also speaks
to the cycle of violence

00:24:12.540 --> 00:24:16.000
and the cycle of risk, were all
fascinating to me in humans.

00:24:16.000 --> 00:24:17.810
So, maybe I'll pause there and then we can

00:24:17.810 --> 00:24:19.020
maybe do a second round
when we start talking

00:24:19.020 --> 00:24:21.580
about the details of the
intergenerational work.

00:24:21.580 --> 00:24:23.550
- Yeah, that sounds great.

00:24:23.550 --> 00:24:25.300
Thanks for just sharing
that context as well.

00:24:25.300 --> 00:24:26.560
I think that's really
helpful for people to

00:24:26.560 --> 00:24:28.240
know what motivated you.

00:24:28.240 --> 00:24:32.190
And it's inspiring again,
to hear that you are

00:24:32.190 --> 00:24:35.800
paying attention to what
you saw in front of you.

00:24:35.800 --> 00:24:36.650
I think that's something that comes

00:24:36.650 --> 00:24:38.450
up in a lot of our conversations, too.

00:24:38.450 --> 00:24:39.950
Especially as we go
through the mental health

00:24:39.950 --> 00:24:42.810
whether the providers are
basically putting their categories

00:24:42.810 --> 00:24:45.410
onto people or listening
to people's stories.

00:24:45.410 --> 00:24:46.620
And it sounds like in a lot of ways

00:24:46.620 --> 00:24:48.511
you were watching and
listening to the stories

00:24:48.511 --> 00:24:50.770
and the experiences that
you saw in front of you

00:24:50.770 --> 00:24:52.890
and saying, "Okay, what
is that leading me to?"

00:24:52.890 --> 00:24:54.020
Rather than the other way around.

00:24:54.020 --> 00:24:55.140
I think that's a really important-

00:24:55.140 --> 00:24:57.700
- Yeah, just to interject,
to follow that up.

00:24:57.700 --> 00:25:00.640
The number of people that
we would see with diagnosis

00:25:00.640 --> 00:25:01.850
there's certainly plenty
with schizophrenia

00:25:01.850 --> 00:25:03.450
and bipolar and severe depression

00:25:03.450 --> 00:25:05.730
but there's a lot of folks
with very severe trauma

00:25:05.730 --> 00:25:08.170
and underlying PTSD that I
think are either misdiagnosed

00:25:08.170 --> 00:25:10.330
or they're putting their
priorities in the wrong place.

00:25:10.330 --> 00:25:11.163
- Yeah, yeah.

00:25:11.163 --> 00:25:11.996
Really important.

00:25:11.996 --> 00:25:13.170
And again, a whole other conversation,

00:25:13.170 --> 00:25:14.260
which we may pull in later,

00:25:14.260 --> 00:25:15.790
but even the disparities that come along

00:25:15.790 --> 00:25:17.850
that as well, even terms
of racial disparities-

00:25:17.850 --> 00:25:20.320
- And that black people
are much more likely to be

00:25:20.320 --> 00:25:21.847
diagnosed with severe mental illness

00:25:21.847 --> 00:25:25.340
than identifying their underlying trauma.

00:25:25.340 --> 00:25:26.760
- Yeah.

00:25:26.760 --> 00:25:28.070
So Bianca, to pivot back to you

00:25:28.070 --> 00:25:28.950
we've pulled out a lot,

00:25:28.950 --> 00:25:30.570
but you shared some of that already from

00:25:30.570 --> 00:25:32.260
your childhood experiences too

00:25:32.260 --> 00:25:34.810
but were there other
components that pulled you

00:25:34.810 --> 00:25:37.888
into this work or things that
are kind of ongoing for you?

00:25:37.888 --> 00:25:40.460
- I think we as scientists,
we walk the line

00:25:40.460 --> 00:25:42.000
between what did the data say

00:25:42.000 --> 00:25:43.680
and what do we know as humans?

00:25:43.680 --> 00:25:44.930
Like, what do we intuit?

00:25:44.930 --> 00:25:49.670
And the data, we're
still parsing this out.

00:25:49.670 --> 00:25:51.850
We're still ironing out
what mechanisms could be.

00:25:51.850 --> 00:25:52.683
And that's okay, right?

00:25:52.683 --> 00:25:54.190
Like I need to have a job.

00:25:54.190 --> 00:25:55.498
So I'm fine with that.

00:25:55.498 --> 00:25:56.331
We don't have all the answers yet

00:25:56.331 --> 00:25:58.410
but we know as humans.

00:25:58.410 --> 00:26:00.650
When we think about finding a partner

00:26:00.650 --> 00:26:02.387
a lot of people ask like,

00:26:02.387 --> 00:26:04.397
"What kind of family do they come from?"

00:26:04.397 --> 00:26:05.547
"What have they gone through?"

00:26:05.547 --> 00:26:06.870
"What are their parents like?"

00:26:06.870 --> 00:26:11.870
We know that certain elements
are imprinted into people.

00:26:12.200 --> 00:26:13.820
We know that people who
have gone through war

00:26:13.820 --> 00:26:16.850
we just intuit that,
potentially there can be

00:26:16.850 --> 00:26:18.360
something that lingers.

00:26:18.360 --> 00:26:19.480
I was in a military family.

00:26:19.480 --> 00:26:22.600
My brother serves in the
United States military.

00:26:22.600 --> 00:26:23.620
Navy.

00:26:23.620 --> 00:26:25.650
And there's certain elements when he was

00:26:25.650 --> 00:26:28.560
out and he came back,
but we would wanna ask

00:26:28.560 --> 00:26:29.920
to see how he was feeling.

00:26:29.920 --> 00:26:32.610
I didn't need data to
know that if you came

00:26:32.610 --> 00:26:34.370
from an abusive household, you were

00:26:34.370 --> 00:26:36.940
sexually or physically
or emotionally abused,

00:26:36.940 --> 00:26:38.220
that that has ramifications.

00:26:38.220 --> 00:26:41.970
I didn't need to look at
your epigenetic changes

00:26:41.970 --> 00:26:43.890
because we know these things as humans.

00:26:43.890 --> 00:26:47.600
And so I think that, in understanding

00:26:47.600 --> 00:26:50.750
that we get this as humans,

00:26:50.750 --> 00:26:52.880
I'm confident that as we get this,

00:26:52.880 --> 00:26:55.100
the data will come rolling.

00:26:55.100 --> 00:26:57.730
And so I think that's
what I try to go back to

00:26:57.730 --> 00:26:59.277
when stuff is hard in the lab and

00:26:59.277 --> 00:27:00.980
you can't really put our finger on

00:27:00.980 --> 00:27:02.560
what it is that we're looking for.

00:27:02.560 --> 00:27:03.930
I just look at society.

00:27:03.930 --> 00:27:05.960
We know these things as human beings.

00:27:05.960 --> 00:27:08.230
And so there's gotta be
an underlying written

00:27:08.230 --> 00:27:11.023
script there for the
behavioral offsets that we see.

00:27:11.860 --> 00:27:14.530
- Yeah, I think that's really
important in so many ways.

00:27:14.530 --> 00:27:15.363
And you're right.

00:27:15.363 --> 00:27:17.070
That line, I'm glad that you brought it up

00:27:17.070 --> 00:27:18.890
because I think we,
sometimes we walk that line

00:27:18.890 --> 00:27:20.990
without acknowledging
that we walked that line.

00:27:20.990 --> 00:27:22.980
I imagine that for both of you,

00:27:22.980 --> 00:27:24.560
that might be something
that's talked about

00:27:24.560 --> 00:27:26.160
more often than in other circles

00:27:26.160 --> 00:27:28.230
but I think it's a principle
that a lot of us should be

00:27:28.230 --> 00:27:31.670
thinking about more explicitly as well.

00:27:31.670 --> 00:27:34.350
And I, even the pieces that you pulled out

00:27:34.350 --> 00:27:37.190
about how to really think
about the data versus

00:27:37.190 --> 00:27:40.230
what we know inherently in
front of us is really important.

00:27:40.230 --> 00:27:42.750
I'm curious if you could expand
a little bit on this idea

00:27:42.750 --> 00:27:46.760
of who's, what's often
characterized as who's at fault

00:27:46.760 --> 00:27:47.820
because you hinted that too.

00:27:47.820 --> 00:27:49.720
'cause you hinted at the
fact that if you know

00:27:49.720 --> 00:27:52.700
your brother's coming back
from a difficult situation

00:27:52.700 --> 00:27:54.520
you all have some grace
in how you're thinking

00:27:54.520 --> 00:27:55.980
about what experiences he had.

00:27:55.980 --> 00:27:58.840
But I think a lot of
times, when we're talking

00:27:58.840 --> 00:27:59.940
about underserved communities,

00:27:59.940 --> 00:28:02.750
there's this narrative
that gets framed that

00:28:02.750 --> 00:28:04.730
while you also just pull yourselves up

00:28:04.730 --> 00:28:06.491
by your bootstraps.

00:28:06.491 --> 00:28:09.180
You know, putting the fault
on the victims in a sense.

00:28:09.180 --> 00:28:11.700
I'm just curious how you think about that

00:28:11.700 --> 00:28:13.380
both in terms of society-wise

00:28:13.380 --> 00:28:15.090
and in terms of your research.

00:28:15.090 --> 00:28:17.400
Bianca, if you wanna just
expand on that a little bit.

00:28:17.400 --> 00:28:18.233
- Of course, yes.

00:28:18.233 --> 00:28:19.337
That's a very important question.

00:28:19.337 --> 00:28:21.700
And I fully support pulling yourself

00:28:21.700 --> 00:28:23.810
up from your bootstraps.

00:28:23.810 --> 00:28:25.641
If you have boots.

00:28:25.641 --> 00:28:28.103
And I think that's the issue.

00:28:28.103 --> 00:28:30.798
When I speak about, or when I think about

00:28:30.798 --> 00:28:33.350
my experience growing up
with my foster siblings

00:28:33.350 --> 00:28:34.183
and I want to make sure

00:28:34.183 --> 00:28:35.016
I'm very clear,

00:28:35.016 --> 00:28:36.720
I'm not speaking from
them or their perspective.

00:28:36.720 --> 00:28:38.640
I'm speaking as myself who had

00:28:38.640 --> 00:28:40.440
two loving, caring parents

00:28:40.440 --> 00:28:41.990
and the things I learned from them.

00:28:41.990 --> 00:28:43.320
I don't ever want to be their voice

00:28:43.320 --> 00:28:45.583
but I do want to share
my experience with them.

00:28:46.830 --> 00:28:48.920
They came from abusive households

00:28:50.190 --> 00:28:53.990
where their parents
tried the best they could

00:28:53.990 --> 00:28:55.820
to manage their children
under the stressors

00:28:55.820 --> 00:28:57.940
they were, stressors they had

00:28:57.940 --> 00:29:00.470
after coming from abusive households.

00:29:00.470 --> 00:29:02.270
And so it's not as if like,

00:29:02.270 --> 00:29:05.470
this just magically
erupted in their lineage.

00:29:05.470 --> 00:29:07.630
This is generation upon generation

00:29:07.630 --> 00:29:10.883
upon generation, of suppression, of abuse,

00:29:12.982 --> 00:29:13.980
of substance abuse.

00:29:13.980 --> 00:29:16.020
But these aren't things
that we can just target

00:29:16.020 --> 00:29:17.363
to one person because,

00:29:18.440 --> 00:29:19.770
and I, for all I know

00:29:19.770 --> 00:29:21.660
some of the people who came

00:29:21.660 --> 00:29:23.550
from these households,
who became my siblings

00:29:23.550 --> 00:29:26.120
could also then have children

00:29:26.120 --> 00:29:27.563
and have to grapple with the way

00:29:27.563 --> 00:29:31.050
that they parent their
kids and what they learned.

00:29:31.050 --> 00:29:35.370
And we see this aligned
in a lot of our research.

00:29:35.370 --> 00:29:37.910
There is a rodent
research that's shown that

00:29:37.910 --> 00:29:41.790
if you have a mother who
smells like peppermint

00:29:42.760 --> 00:29:45.130
and then you have her offspring

00:29:45.130 --> 00:29:46.290
and you shock her offspring

00:29:46.290 --> 00:29:48.300
while she's next to the
mom with peppermint.

00:29:48.300 --> 00:29:50.580
Even though the animals felt shock,

00:29:50.580 --> 00:29:52.490
so it felt pain surrounding the mom,

00:29:52.490 --> 00:29:54.730
it still prefers peppermint.

00:29:54.730 --> 00:29:56.177
And so there's something
innate about saying,

00:29:56.177 --> 00:29:58.280
"Although I know that this
is not a good situation.

00:29:58.280 --> 00:29:59.820
I do wanna go back to this parent.

00:29:59.820 --> 00:30:02.047
I do wanna go back to my mother."

00:30:02.980 --> 00:30:05.754
And how much of that can
be parsed out societally

00:30:05.754 --> 00:30:07.470
by saying, "Your mother was horrible.

00:30:07.470 --> 00:30:08.630
You cannot be like this person.

00:30:08.630 --> 00:30:09.463
Your father was horrible.

00:30:09.463 --> 00:30:10.540
You cannot be like this person."

00:30:10.540 --> 00:30:11.957
And having the pull to love.

00:30:11.957 --> 00:30:15.310
And so I do find that's something that I

00:30:15.310 --> 00:30:17.200
it's hard to parse out in the science

00:30:17.200 --> 00:30:18.890
because you want something clean, crisp

00:30:18.890 --> 00:30:20.630
like the data say this.

00:30:20.630 --> 00:30:22.540
But there are emotions
involved with parenthood.

00:30:22.540 --> 00:30:26.930
There are emotions involved
with generations to come.

00:30:26.930 --> 00:30:28.860
And what that looks like in rodents

00:30:28.860 --> 00:30:30.850
which is what I use to study in my

00:30:30.850 --> 00:30:31.870
look at my question,

00:30:31.870 --> 00:30:34.298
it's hard to not to anthropomorphize that.

00:30:34.298 --> 00:30:36.247
- Yeah, that's a really good example.

00:30:36.247 --> 00:30:37.080
Go ahead, Kerry.

00:30:37.080 --> 00:30:38.767
- Sorry, that was
beautifully said, Bianca.

00:30:38.767 --> 00:30:39.600
You know, it's kind of

00:30:39.600 --> 00:30:41.147
we're bringing it back to the big story.

00:30:41.147 --> 00:30:43.610
And I really see that
that's so much an issue

00:30:43.610 --> 00:30:45.080
with institutional racism.

00:30:45.080 --> 00:30:47.093
I think you can even
have people meaning well

00:30:47.093 --> 00:30:48.200
(indistinct)

00:30:48.200 --> 00:30:49.393
they don't understand, we don't understand

00:30:49.393 --> 00:30:51.623
because there's just so
many false equivalencies.

00:30:52.490 --> 00:30:53.700
Doesn't matter what happened
to you when you're early

00:30:53.700 --> 00:30:55.540
we're now giving you school opportunity.

00:30:55.540 --> 00:30:57.680
We're giving you some food.

00:30:57.680 --> 00:30:58.541
We're giving a headstart.

00:30:58.541 --> 00:30:59.930
We're giving a job.

00:30:59.930 --> 00:31:01.180
Why aren't things better?

00:31:01.180 --> 00:31:04.260
And there's just a lack of appreciation

00:31:04.260 --> 00:31:06.690
that these things that
we know are critical

00:31:06.690 --> 00:31:07.603
for our developmental, like you said,

00:31:07.603 --> 00:31:09.280
but even with the topic of this,

00:31:09.280 --> 00:31:11.230
potentially even intergenerational

00:31:11.230 --> 00:31:12.300
that we've got to be more

00:31:12.300 --> 00:31:14.240
you can't just have a headstart program.

00:31:14.240 --> 00:31:17.060
You can't just have a job opportunity.

00:31:17.060 --> 00:31:20.260
If the emotional building
blocks aren't there

00:31:20.260 --> 00:31:22.650
in addition to the new opportunities,

00:31:22.650 --> 00:31:23.483
it's not gonna work.

00:31:23.483 --> 00:31:25.100
So, you don't have the boots

00:31:25.100 --> 00:31:27.280
even though it may look
like you have them.

00:31:27.280 --> 00:31:28.113
So there's that.

00:31:28.113 --> 00:31:28.946
Due to false equivalencies of,

00:31:28.946 --> 00:31:29.840
"Okay we're giving them now"

00:31:29.840 --> 00:31:31.340
but they're just not the same.

00:31:31.340 --> 00:31:32.480
And we haven't, as a society,

00:31:32.480 --> 00:31:34.755
learned that or figured
out what to do about that.

00:31:34.755 --> 00:31:36.800
- Yeah, I think that's so important.

00:31:36.800 --> 00:31:38.700
And I appreciate the fact that you're

00:31:38.700 --> 00:31:39.660
pulling that out too,

00:31:39.660 --> 00:31:40.600
Both of you, that we have

00:31:40.600 --> 00:31:42.980
we can see those society-wise as well.

00:31:42.980 --> 00:31:45.000
We don't have to wait for all the data

00:31:45.000 --> 00:31:46.310
to know that that's true.

00:31:46.310 --> 00:31:47.420
But even at the same time, Kerry.

00:31:47.420 --> 00:31:49.500
If you could just expand on what the data

00:31:49.500 --> 00:31:51.160
has started to show us about

00:31:51.160 --> 00:31:54.290
how those processes are actually happening

00:31:54.290 --> 00:31:56.370
in terms of what Bianca
already brought up.

00:31:56.370 --> 00:31:58.998
- Sure, so one of the things,

00:31:58.998 --> 00:32:00.840
sort of two brief stories

00:32:00.840 --> 00:32:02.500
that we've been most excited about

00:32:02.500 --> 00:32:04.160
is this concept of epigenetics.

00:32:04.160 --> 00:32:06.720
I think most people are familiar
with the idea of genetics.

00:32:06.720 --> 00:32:08.320
That's what you're born with, your DNA,

00:32:08.320 --> 00:32:09.440
it's in your cells.

00:32:09.440 --> 00:32:11.260
And, you know, essentially
we all come from

00:32:11.260 --> 00:32:14.000
a mix of our moms' and our dads' genes.

00:32:14.000 --> 00:32:15.880
But over the last couple decades,

00:32:15.880 --> 00:32:17.030
it's really understood that there's

00:32:17.030 --> 00:32:18.260
this concept of epigenetics.

00:32:18.260 --> 00:32:20.060
How the genes can be altered.

00:32:20.060 --> 00:32:21.890
And data, for a number of years

00:32:21.890 --> 00:32:24.830
have suggested that developmental trauma

00:32:24.830 --> 00:32:26.960
be that neglect, abuse, physical,

00:32:26.960 --> 00:32:28.170
sexual, emotional abuse

00:32:28.170 --> 00:32:31.160
that's more associated with
all bad things basically

00:32:31.160 --> 00:32:34.720
Whether it'd be medical, psychological,

00:32:34.720 --> 00:32:36.590
substance abuse, risk, et cetera.

00:32:36.590 --> 00:32:38.617
And we're starting to understand that

00:32:39.530 --> 00:32:41.750
marks, both in your blood and in the brain

00:32:41.750 --> 00:32:43.830
the ways in which the
genes have been altered

00:32:43.830 --> 00:32:45.700
via this early developmental experience

00:32:45.700 --> 00:32:47.430
through this epigenetic term.

00:32:47.430 --> 00:32:48.293
And so that's one way
that we're starting to

00:32:48.293 --> 00:32:51.420
understand how the biology
of development may hold

00:32:51.420 --> 00:32:54.019
on and be kind of stuck
there during adulthood.

00:32:54.019 --> 00:32:56.060
And one, for example,
that we've been involved

00:32:56.060 --> 00:32:57.630
with a lot is a gene that's involved

00:32:57.630 --> 00:32:59.720
in the stress regulatory system

00:32:59.720 --> 00:33:03.150
and epigenetic changes
in some of these genes

00:33:03.150 --> 00:33:06.110
alter how the body and the
mind respond to stress.

00:33:06.110 --> 00:33:07.470
And that looks very different

00:33:07.470 --> 00:33:09.320
if you've had childhood trauma versus not

00:33:09.320 --> 00:33:12.270
and likely very much
tunes the stress system

00:33:12.270 --> 00:33:15.370
and the body and the brain
very differently in adulthood.

00:33:15.370 --> 00:33:17.390
And then the work where Bianca and I met

00:33:17.390 --> 00:33:20.850
and share scientific excitement is

00:33:20.850 --> 00:33:22.810
we both start in the olfactory system

00:33:22.810 --> 00:33:23.970
I'd been working for a few years

00:33:23.970 --> 00:33:25.330
and then had transitioned over to

00:33:25.330 --> 00:33:27.070
studying fear and amygdala.

00:33:27.070 --> 00:33:28.700
But for all the reasons Bianca said,

00:33:28.700 --> 00:33:30.370
the olfactory system is so fascinating

00:33:30.370 --> 00:33:32.110
and one of which is
because we can structure

00:33:32.110 --> 00:33:33.900
we have molecular tools that allow us to

00:33:33.900 --> 00:33:37.340
see the structural
organization of how smell works

00:33:37.340 --> 00:33:38.790
in the nose and in the brain.

00:33:38.790 --> 00:33:40.980
The part of the brain
called the olfactory bowl.

00:33:40.980 --> 00:33:43.530
And we found, based on really inspired by

00:33:43.530 --> 00:33:45.890
work by many scientists over decades,

00:33:45.890 --> 00:33:48.400
where they showed that if
you had early experiences,

00:33:48.400 --> 00:33:50.380
it changed the structure of the brain

00:33:50.380 --> 00:33:52.240
and you had different sensory systems.

00:33:52.240 --> 00:33:53.710
And we were able to show that

00:33:53.710 --> 00:33:55.650
if you even had an adult,

00:33:55.650 --> 00:33:57.540
because of the, what we know now is

00:33:57.540 --> 00:33:58.890
if there's turnover constantly

00:33:58.890 --> 00:33:59.750
in the olfactory system.

00:33:59.750 --> 00:34:01.450
That's why if somebody
has a cold or COVID now

00:34:01.450 --> 00:34:03.510
and loses smell, eventually it comes back

00:34:03.510 --> 00:34:05.330
because the cells grow back.

00:34:05.330 --> 00:34:07.630
So that suggested there's
this ongoing plasticity

00:34:07.630 --> 00:34:09.610
in the olfactory system
that may be different

00:34:09.610 --> 00:34:11.150
than other sensory systems.

00:34:11.150 --> 00:34:14.720
And so we found that if you
train an animal to be afraid of

00:34:14.720 --> 00:34:17.300
so you can shock them with a cherry smell

00:34:17.300 --> 00:34:20.480
that the sense specific
molecular markers of cherry

00:34:20.480 --> 00:34:22.980
actually increase in the nose
and increase in the brain.

00:34:22.980 --> 00:34:24.820
So you have an increased representation

00:34:24.820 --> 00:34:27.720
and an increased behavioral
sensitization of that cherry

00:34:27.720 --> 00:34:29.308
and the molecular mechanisms

00:34:29.308 --> 00:34:31.040
that are starting to be understood.

00:34:31.040 --> 00:34:33.610
And a brilliant postdoc
named Brian Dias in the lab

00:34:33.610 --> 00:34:35.540
wanted to look at this in broader ways

00:34:35.540 --> 00:34:37.930
and basically almost randomly

00:34:38.880 --> 00:34:39.880
he planned it.

00:34:39.880 --> 00:34:42.570
I wasn't very surprised
and have found that

00:34:42.570 --> 00:34:44.170
if you train, if you shock

00:34:44.170 --> 00:34:46.050
basically traumatize a father

00:34:46.050 --> 00:34:49.160
before they give birth
to this cherry smell

00:34:49.160 --> 00:34:52.153
the offspring actually are
more sensitive to cherry

00:34:52.153 --> 00:34:54.990
have more representation
of cherry in the nose

00:34:54.990 --> 00:34:57.170
and more representation
of cherry in the brain.

00:34:57.170 --> 00:34:59.120
And he showed that in
lots of different ways

00:34:59.120 --> 00:35:01.120
that even if you just take the sperm

00:35:01.120 --> 00:35:03.000
of the father after
they've been traumatized

00:35:03.000 --> 00:35:04.790
and do what we call
in-vitro fertilization,

00:35:04.790 --> 00:35:05.870
take it to a different place,

00:35:05.870 --> 00:35:07.900
no behavioral interaction at all

00:35:07.900 --> 00:35:10.670
the next generation
still had these molecular

00:35:10.670 --> 00:35:12.960
and structural and behavioral marks

00:35:12.960 --> 00:35:16.000
of increased sensitization
to this trauma smell

00:35:16.000 --> 00:35:18.090
suggesting that at least
one sensory system,

00:35:18.090 --> 00:35:20.500
the olfactory system, is able to hold onto

00:35:20.500 --> 00:35:22.630
somehow some of its olfactory information

00:35:22.630 --> 00:35:25.940
that is important across generations.

00:35:25.940 --> 00:35:29.340
And that has so many implications
that we can talk about.

00:35:29.340 --> 00:35:31.030
So my way of getting to know Bianca

00:35:31.030 --> 00:35:32.690
was I heard these rumors
that there was somebody

00:35:32.690 --> 00:35:34.587
from the Axel lab who'd
replicated some of the work

00:35:34.587 --> 00:35:36.007
and we all got very excited

00:35:36.007 --> 00:35:36.917
the next thing you know.

00:35:36.917 --> 00:35:39.210
A year and a half, be anxiously awaiting

00:35:39.210 --> 00:35:41.016
to meet her to hear more about it.

00:35:41.016 --> 00:35:42.474
- Yeah.

00:35:42.474 --> 00:35:44.290
I mean, that speaks to the
scientific endeavor too.

00:35:44.290 --> 00:35:45.230
And I'm curious actually,

00:35:45.230 --> 00:35:47.680
to even piggyback on that and see

00:35:47.680 --> 00:35:48.820
just to hear from both from your pers-

00:35:48.820 --> 00:35:51.878
How did people first respond to the work?

00:35:51.878 --> 00:35:53.370
(Kerry chuckles)

00:35:53.370 --> 00:35:54.250
- I'll let Kerry answer that

00:35:54.250 --> 00:35:55.670
'cause I heard that in parallel

00:35:55.670 --> 00:35:57.960
as I was making a decision
as to what I was going to

00:35:57.960 --> 00:36:00.067
focus my postdoctoral work on

00:36:00.067 --> 00:36:01.643
and my postdoctoral advisor was like,

00:36:01.643 --> 00:36:04.398
"Are you sure you wanna study
this? Because I know that-

00:36:04.398 --> 00:36:06.510
(voices overlapping)

00:36:06.510 --> 00:36:07.585
Run away!

00:36:07.585 --> 00:36:09.770
(laughs)

00:36:09.770 --> 00:36:11.120
But it goes back to what I was saying.

00:36:11.120 --> 00:36:14.200
We know these things intuitively as humans

00:36:14.200 --> 00:36:16.360
but it's so hard to convince people that

00:36:16.360 --> 00:36:19.050
the data of what we're
showing is relevant.

00:36:19.050 --> 00:36:20.567
So I'll let Kerry answer
that question because I'm-

00:36:20.567 --> 00:36:23.080
- Yeah, I mean the brief
answer is skepticism.

00:36:23.080 --> 00:36:24.660
Lots and lots of skepticism.

00:36:24.660 --> 00:36:26.260
There's, you know, other work by people

00:36:26.260 --> 00:36:28.250
like Isabelle Mansuy and Tracy Bale

00:36:28.250 --> 00:36:29.410
in Germany and Maryland

00:36:29.410 --> 00:36:30.410
who'd done other great work

00:36:30.410 --> 00:36:32.320
concerning intergenerational stress.

00:36:32.320 --> 00:36:34.340
Problem is, the stress
system's really complicated

00:36:34.340 --> 00:36:35.465
and it's kind of nuanced

00:36:35.465 --> 00:36:36.670
and there's not really very

00:36:36.670 --> 00:36:38.710
specific molecules all the time.

00:36:38.710 --> 00:36:41.310
And I think what's the olfactory
system has going for us

00:36:41.310 --> 00:36:46.220
and why this story from all
of our groups is so powerful

00:36:46.220 --> 00:36:48.660
is that we do have these
very precise markers.

00:36:48.660 --> 00:36:50.910
And you can, you have
these molecular markers

00:36:50.910 --> 00:36:51.743
for one smell versus another smell.

00:36:51.743 --> 00:36:54.640
So you have this built-in
control to show that

00:36:54.640 --> 00:36:56.200
you can have this inheritance that

00:36:56.200 --> 00:36:58.570
affects one smell system but not the

00:36:58.570 --> 00:37:00.220
so it's one odor, but not another.

00:37:00.220 --> 00:37:01.620
And that allowed us to overcome it.

00:37:01.620 --> 00:37:03.710
But all the skepticism certainly

00:37:03.710 --> 00:37:05.700
made the paper better
and made us do a lot more

00:37:05.700 --> 00:37:07.890
of the gazillion extra experiments

00:37:07.890 --> 00:37:09.500
to really show that this
is a real phenomenon.

00:37:09.500 --> 00:37:10.333
We don't know what it means

00:37:10.333 --> 00:37:11.520
but we know it's in the sperm

00:37:11.520 --> 00:37:13.790
and we think it's epigenetics.

00:37:13.790 --> 00:37:15.390
But one kind of fun example is,

00:37:15.390 --> 00:37:17.360
so this comes back to
this historical thing

00:37:17.360 --> 00:37:18.440
of Darwin versus Lamarck.

00:37:18.440 --> 00:37:20.760
What are you inherit
versus what can you change.

00:37:20.760 --> 00:37:21.593
And everybody was saying,

00:37:21.593 --> 00:37:22.426
"Oh, this is Lamarck."

00:37:22.426 --> 00:37:23.340
And so we threw that out.

00:37:23.340 --> 00:37:24.260
The problem is we didn't know about

00:37:24.260 --> 00:37:25.320
epigenetics and we didn't know

00:37:25.320 --> 00:37:27.300
that probably both are true at some level.

00:37:27.300 --> 00:37:28.330
And we were trying not to

00:37:28.330 --> 00:37:29.640
so we were trying to avoid that issue

00:37:29.640 --> 00:37:31.080
and just talk about the data

00:37:31.080 --> 00:37:32.248
and then nature neuroscience word came out

00:37:32.248 --> 00:37:34.886
without telling us, putting
Lamarck on the cover.

00:37:34.886 --> 00:37:37.490
(laughs)

00:37:37.490 --> 00:37:38.987
We're trying to avoid that issue.

00:37:38.987 --> 00:37:39.820
- Yeah, no choice.

00:37:39.820 --> 00:37:41.850
(laughs)

00:37:41.850 --> 00:37:43.320
That's how it works sometimes.

00:37:43.320 --> 00:37:44.490
Yeah, that's really helpful.

00:37:44.490 --> 00:37:46.000
I think to know that obviously

00:37:46.000 --> 00:37:46.860
it wasn't easy at the time

00:37:46.860 --> 00:37:49.440
but to know the context
too, and the pushback.

00:37:49.440 --> 00:37:51.180
How would you say, and maybe Bianca

00:37:51.180 --> 00:37:52.600
you can talk about this.

00:37:52.600 --> 00:37:54.780
Was that mirrored on the societal side?

00:37:54.780 --> 00:37:58.220
Like how the society as a collective

00:37:58.220 --> 00:38:00.003
respond to the work?

00:38:00.003 --> 00:38:02.560
- I think that comes back
to our job as scientists

00:38:02.560 --> 00:38:04.360
by taking the data and saying that

00:38:04.360 --> 00:38:06.190
these are the data that we have.

00:38:06.190 --> 00:38:08.640
We're in the process of interpreting them.

00:38:08.640 --> 00:38:10.140
And I think that comes back to,

00:38:10.140 --> 00:38:11.620
I don't think I would say I know this

00:38:11.620 --> 00:38:12.580
comes back to the fact that

00:38:12.580 --> 00:38:14.300
if I'm flipping through a paper

00:38:14.300 --> 00:38:15.520
or I'm flipping through a book

00:38:15.520 --> 00:38:16.753
or I'm not a scientist.

00:38:16.753 --> 00:38:18.390
I'm just reading the New York Times

00:38:18.390 --> 00:38:19.223
or something of the sort

00:38:19.223 --> 00:38:21.620
and I see, "You've had trauma in your past

00:38:21.620 --> 00:38:23.420
and now you're ruined for life

00:38:23.420 --> 00:38:25.330
because these epigenetic markers have

00:38:25.330 --> 00:38:26.830
now been ingrained in your system."

00:38:26.830 --> 00:38:29.700
And so you're just like,
you're out of luck.

00:38:29.700 --> 00:38:32.690
I would be pretty offended
and I'd be angered

00:38:32.690 --> 00:38:33.556
because this person,

00:38:33.556 --> 00:38:35.960
this scientist doesn't know who I am.

00:38:35.960 --> 00:38:37.170
They don't know how I deal with things.

00:38:37.170 --> 00:38:40.570
I think that's a part that we
need to definitely highlight.

00:38:40.570 --> 00:38:43.300
We don't know exactly
what we're looking at.

00:38:43.300 --> 00:38:45.640
We could be looking at
something that could align

00:38:45.640 --> 00:38:48.130
with PTSD, increased sensitivity

00:38:48.130 --> 00:38:49.940
to a non-threatening cue.

00:38:49.940 --> 00:38:51.718
We could be looking at resilience,

00:38:51.718 --> 00:38:54.690
the ability of the nose
to then sense an odor

00:38:54.690 --> 00:38:57.260
that in the past was traumatizing.

00:38:57.260 --> 00:38:59.490
But now, I'm warned about it earlier

00:38:59.490 --> 00:39:00.410
because I have more neurons

00:39:00.410 --> 00:39:02.350
that are going to say, hmm,

00:39:02.350 --> 00:39:04.360
or learn more quickly.

00:39:04.360 --> 00:39:06.640
And so the data remained the same

00:39:06.640 --> 00:39:10.093
but those two explanations
are how we interpret the data.

00:39:12.760 --> 00:39:15.267
My goal as a scientist is to
take the data that we have

00:39:15.267 --> 00:39:19.070
and really interpret it
and with multiple facets

00:39:19.070 --> 00:39:21.140
in the way that benefits society.

00:39:21.140 --> 00:39:23.610
And so I'm very hesitant
when we speak about trauma,

00:39:23.610 --> 00:39:24.720
just trauma, trauma

00:39:24.720 --> 00:39:26.060
because that can be really traumatizing

00:39:26.060 --> 00:39:27.130
especially for someone like me

00:39:27.130 --> 00:39:29.741
who's like a black woman,
first generation SES.

00:39:29.741 --> 00:39:32.440
I don't wanna just swallow
the fact that like,

00:39:32.440 --> 00:39:35.430
okay, I have generations upon generations

00:39:35.430 --> 00:39:37.870
of slavery-induced trauma.

00:39:37.870 --> 00:39:41.310
And now I just can't
live life functionally.

00:39:41.310 --> 00:39:43.190
Maybe there's an element of resilience

00:39:43.190 --> 00:39:45.629
that can be inherited as well.

00:39:45.629 --> 00:39:47.250
And just remembering that, I think

00:39:47.250 --> 00:39:50.110
gives me energy and
joy, and I do wanna tell

00:39:50.110 --> 00:39:51.580
I try to make sure that the community

00:39:51.580 --> 00:39:53.300
and society at large knows,

00:39:53.300 --> 00:39:54.581
these are just the data.

00:39:54.581 --> 00:39:56.005
It could mean something bad.

00:39:56.005 --> 00:39:57.757
It could mean something amazing.

00:39:57.757 --> 00:39:59.956
And we're in the process
of figuring that out.

00:39:59.956 --> 00:40:00.789
- Yeah.

00:40:00.789 --> 00:40:01.622
That's so good-

00:40:01.622 --> 00:40:02.480
- Can I just add to that too?

00:40:02.480 --> 00:40:03.840
I think it was so beautifully said.

00:40:03.840 --> 00:40:05.450
You know, one of the
things I wanna make sure

00:40:05.450 --> 00:40:06.343
that I get in and I always try to

00:40:06.343 --> 00:40:08.480
when I talk about this is the latest work

00:40:08.480 --> 00:40:09.750
that I think is so exciting is that

00:40:09.750 --> 00:40:11.370
if you take the same fathers,

00:40:11.370 --> 00:40:13.210
you traumatized them to the cherry,

00:40:13.210 --> 00:40:14.220
but then you extinguish them.

00:40:14.220 --> 00:40:15.830
You essentially give them exposure therapy

00:40:15.830 --> 00:40:17.640
just like we do with PTSD.

00:40:17.640 --> 00:40:20.470
And then you make them
offspring looked just

00:40:20.470 --> 00:40:21.910
like nothing ever happened.

00:40:21.910 --> 00:40:23.850
So what this suggests is that

00:40:23.850 --> 00:40:25.810
all of these processes are plastic.

00:40:25.810 --> 00:40:28.310
And if we know the right way to intervene

00:40:28.310 --> 00:40:30.760
in the way to help
further build resilience

00:40:30.760 --> 00:40:32.830
that these are plastic, they're not fate.

00:40:32.830 --> 00:40:34.240
And that's, I think really important.

00:40:34.240 --> 00:40:36.170
- Yeah, really important points.

00:40:36.170 --> 00:40:37.147
And beautiful segue too because

00:40:37.147 --> 00:40:39.200
one of the things that often we try

00:40:39.200 --> 00:40:40.500
